  "id": "2026-09-16-history-configuration-final-pass",
  "scope": "history",
  "kind": "review",
  "date": "2026-09-16",
  "question": "After accounting for Plate descriptor structure and types, should Plite gain native configuration so the Plate history state/getter adapter can disappear?",
  "model": "GPT-5 / Codex",
  "method": "best-api-review/history-v2",
  "trigger": "User requests a final pass after correcting that Plate still enriches the native plugin with structure and types.",
  "requirements": [
    "Preserve Plate-specific descriptor structure, authoring methods, inference, terminal configuration and scoped per-editor state.",
    "Preserve Plite's exact descriptor model, immutable factory inputs, raw transactional reconfiguration and one installed history recorder.",
    "Require an independent current job before adding native initialState, a store, configure semantics or a generic factory adapter.",
    "Compare unchanged re-export, static native adoption, factory-only Plate composition, generic native configuration and the current explicit bridge."
  ],
  "evidenceReuse": "Rechecked the live Plate constructor, BasePlugin contract, native descriptor adoption test, lowering boundary, HistoryPlugin wrapper, Plite HistoryOptions closure reads, activation cleanup, raw transactional reconfiguration tests, public history guide and Plate/Plite doctrine. Targeted non-test search still found construction-time Plate configuration but no application live store mutation. Reused the adopted replay/grouping receipts only as baseline behavior evidence; no tests were rerun.",
  "summary": "Stop the Plite configuration redesign and keep the explicit Plate history adapter. The prior Pursue verdict still overclaimed after preserving the wrapper: static native adoption cannot infer an absent Plate state schema or retroactively parameterize history(options). Plite deliberately owns exact descriptors with immutable factory inputs, while Plate owns enriched descriptors and mutable per-editor initialState/store parameters. The getters are the honest bridge that preserves one recorder and live Plate state. A generic Plite state/configuration model adds a second contract for one adapter; raw transactional plugin replacement is not equivalent. Repeated default literals are at most internal constant drift, not a public API or architecture opportunity.",
  "verdict": "stop",
  "previous": "2026-09-16-history-plate-adaptation",
  "relation": "reverses",
  "alternatives": [
    "Keep the current explicit adapter: strongest direction. Plate declares its narrower runtime state, and getters expose live per-editor values to the one native history descriptor without another recorder.",
    "Unchanged native re-export: rejected because it loses Plate structure, authoring methods, terminal configuration, store and inferred contract.",
    "Generic static native-to-Plate adaptation: current .extend(RawPlugin) already adopts native capabilities and identity, but cannot infer an absent Plate state schema or rebuild the closure captured by history(options). A helper would hide rather than delete the bridge.",
    "Add native initialState/store/configure: rejected because Plite construction inputs intentionally live in factory closures. One history adapter does not earn a second runtime configuration model in the substrate.",
    "Use history(options) only in Plate: rejected because it snapshots construction-time inputs and abandons Plate's standard initialState/configure/store contract.",
    "Use tx.plugins.reconfigure for live settings: rejected because it replaces a raw descriptor transactionally, while Plate topology is fixed and history activation resets automatic grouping time.",
    "Centralize internal history defaults: possible private cleanup because 100 is repeated in Plite history initialization and Plate defaults, but it does not alter ownership or justify public API work."
  ],
  "proofLimits": "Bounded source-only final pass, not a new audit of all ten history units. No product code changed and no product test, benchmark, declaration or browser proof was run. Existing tests prove current live store reads and raw transactional reconfiguration behavior; targeted application search did not establish a production live-setting mutation. The Stop verdict rests on distinct authoring/lifetime contracts and lack of a second native configuration consumer, not a performance claim. Existing adopted/verified receipts continue to apply only to the installed replay/grouping design.",
